It occurs to me that this is a great introductory  Arduino, project.  It uses a Hall detector.  
This implies that there is a magnet on the pendulum.  But it is wonderful.  The detector is very forgiving.
The Arduino uno board has only three wires: ground, 5 volts and the sensor pin.
I am unable to wake up Fritzing.  But the wiring is easy.  
The Hall detector is a magnetic detector.  A magnet is put on the pendulum. 
With a neodymium magnet the detector sees the pendulum at even half an inch away.  The platform is very forgiving.
The included “.ino “ file is configured to average 300 numbers and only print after 300, about five minutes. 
So, as written, it prints a few lines every five minutes.  A typical output is 1.076546.  This is seconds. 
The last two digits are pretty much noise in this application.  Aas the number is printed, I take them away by diving by 100.
This is a pretty cool project and I hope it contributes in some way.

